He uses magic to try to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.360
a prophecy? Isn’t that sort of like&nbsp;
begging for ironic narrative retribution?

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.120
I don’t know what to tell you. Every baby looks&nbsp;
like a nail when all you’ve got is a magic hammer.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:05.840
A hammer would have worked.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:06.480
MOVING ON.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.401
Because Harry’s mom sacrifices herself to save&nbsp;
him, Harry is granted powerful magical protection.

00:01:11.401 --> 00:01:12.242
That's correct.

00:01:12.242 --> 00:01:14.880
Just like how that secret service&nbsp;
agent died protecting President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.320
Truman and now he's our immortal,&nbsp;
bulletproof president for life.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:19.760
Sure.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.400
Voldemort gets turned into a problem for later.&nbsp;
Also, Harry winds up with some of Voldemort’s soul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.520
in his tummy. So now he can talk to snakes.&nbsp;
Oh, and his forehead glows when orcs are near.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.331
Close enough.

00:01:30.331 --> 00:01:34.240
Harry grows up with his aunt and uncle and cousin,&nbsp;
who are the bad guys from every Roald Dahl book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.240
until his 11th birthday, when he finds out&nbsp;
that he has magic powers and goes shopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.160
for a stick that shoots fire and can kill&nbsp;
people. Then it's off to elementary school.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.560
Book one ends with Harry and his friends doing the&nbsp;
British version of Legends of the Hidden Temple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.480
and Harry fighting the back of his teacher's head.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:51.600
Mmhmm.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.560
Book two is a classic murder mystery where the&nbsp;
culprit is a racist snake. Book three is a classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.520
British time travel caper in the tradition of&nbsp;
Doctor Who, and the worst episodes of Doctor Who.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.400
This is also the book where the fans get upset&nbsp;
when they find out that the rat that Ron has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.640
been sharing a bed with for years was actually&nbsp;
a middle-aged man. But what they never seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:16.560
mention is whether it would have been less weird&nbsp;
if he’d just been sleeping next to a regular rat. Right?

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.920
Yeah, marginally less weird.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.560
Book four marks an important&nbsp;
tonal shift for Harry. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.760
being chosen to compete in what’s&nbsp;
essentially the magic Olympics…

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.440
